.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- execution-target device for gpu(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/gpuclone
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I gpu
device is the native backend
.IR gpu (2)'s
own
.B Backend.apply
talks to when configured for
.BR "device gpu" ;
scripts and most Limbo programs should reach it through
.IR gpu (2)
rather than opening it directly.
.PP
Unlike
.IR ssl (3),
.I gpu
binds flat into
.B /dev
(matching
.IR audio (3)'s
own
.BR /dev/audio / /dev/audioctl
convention, not
.IR ssl (3)'s
own numbered-subdirectory one) \(em its one file is
.BR /dev/gpuclone ,
not a
.B clone
file under a
.B /dev/gpu
directory. Opening
.B /dev/gpuclone
allocates one handle: matrix state (the uploaded
.IR sparse (2)
CSR arrays) and the pending response live on that one open channel,
which also serves both reads and writes \(em there is no separate
.B ctl
/
.B data
split, since every message on a handle is naturally one request
immediately followed by one response.
.PP
The wire format is binary.
Integers are 32-bit and values IEEE754 single, both big-endian, which
is what Limbo's
.B math->export_int
and
.B math->export_real32
produce, so a caller needs no hand-rolled marshalling.
Single precision throughout, because the hardware path is f32-only
anyway (see the
.B P
request).
Each write is one request; the following read returns its response.
Three request shapes, each starting with a one-letter tag:
.EX
	P
.EE
The following read returns the precision a matvec on this handle would
.B actually
deliver, which for this protocol is always
.BR f32 .
Not cosmetic \(em a caller needing f64 must know that
.B before
trusting a result.
This is what
.IR gpu (2)'s
.B precision
verb queries.
.PP
The answer is unconditional, and deliberately so.
A hardware backend is f32-only anyway (Metal has no
.BR double ),
but even with no hardware backend linked at all \(em when every matvec
falls through to the portable CPU loop below, which is written in
.B double
\(em the
.B U
and
.B X
requests still carry their values as 32-bit IEEE754 singles.
The vector handed back has therefore been through f32 twice whatever
computed it, so reporting
.B f64
on the fallback path (as this device once did) told a caller who asked
for double that it was getting double while quietly handing back
single \(em precisely the failure this request exists to prevent.
Carrying genuine f64 needs new request tags with 8-byte values.
.EX
	U  n  nnz  rowptr[n+1]  colidx[nnz]  val[nnz]
.EE
Uploads an
.IR n \(mu n
CSR matrix with
.I nnz
nonzeros, replacing any previous upload on this handle.
The following read returns
.B OK
on success, or an error.
.EX
	X  x[n]
.EE
Computes
.IR y = Ax
against the uploaded matrix.
The following read returns
.I y
as
.I n
raw single-precision values.
.PP
Text was tried first and was not merely slow: formatting a whole CSR
matrix as decimal exhausted the Limbo heap at a 16x16x16 mesh, long
before the GPU was the limit, and the per-iteration vector exchange
cost more than the arithmetic it carried.
.PP
Closing the handle frees its matrix state.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION
The numeric kernel is not this device's own job. It dispatches through
three nullable hooks \(em
.BR gpuhwupload ,
.BR gpuhwspmv ,
.B gpuhwfree
\(em exactly the convention
.I devdraw.c
already uses for
.BR gpudrawfillpoly3d / g :
a platform backend that has real GPU compute assigns them at load
time, and everywhere they are nil every matvec silently uses this
device's own portable CPU loop instead. Adding or removing a hardware
backend therefore changes nothing about this device's Styx surface,
and no platform
.B #ifdef
appears in it.
.PP
On this platform
.RB ( win-gpu.m ,
named by the
.B gpu
line in the
.B emu-cocoa
device manifest, the same way
.B draw
names
.BR win-cocoa )
those hooks are a real
.B MTLComputePipelineState
running one GPU thread per matrix row. The matrix buffers are uploaded
once per matrix and stay device-resident; only the x and y vectors
cross per matvec \(em which is the whole reason offloading a Krylov
solve pays off, and what
.IR gpu (2)'s
.B resident
verb refers to. That file deliberately includes no Inferno headers at
all: the hooks are plain C types on both sides, so it needs neither
.I dat.h
nor the
.BR Point / Rect
renaming
.I win-cocoa.m
has to do.
.PP
Metal Shading Language has no
.BR double ,
so the hardware path is inherently f32, converting on the way in and
out. That is a hardware property, not a shortcut, and it is why the
.B P
request above exists: rather than quietly returning single precision
to a caller who asked for double,
.IR gpu (2)
asks this device what it would really compute in and declines to use
it when that is not good enough. The effect is directly observable
\(em an
.IR fem (2)
Poisson solve with
.B "backend gpu precision f32"
converges in 9 CG iterations to a residual around 1e\-11, against 7
iterations and 1e\-17 for the same solve on the CPU, with the two
solutions agreeing to about 4e\-8: f32 epsilon, exactly as expected.
.PP
Whether offloading is worth it depends entirely on problem size, so
measure rather than assume \(em
.IR gpubench (1)
does exactly this comparison.
A
.IR fem (2)
Poisson solve on this machine, cpu f64 against gpu f32:
.EX
	mesh      nodes   cpu f64            gpu f32
	                  solve   us/iter    solve   us/iter
	12x12x12   2197    0-1ms      -      3-4ms     176
	16x16x16   4913    1-1ms     45      5-8ms     200
	20x20x20   9261    2-3ms     71     6-12ms     188
	24x24x24  15625    5-6ms    147     7-18ms     179
	28x28x28  21952  10-11ms    250    10-23ms     222
	30x30x30  27000  13-15ms    310    13-25ms     250
	32x32x32  32768  17-18ms    378    15-28ms     288
.EE
Best of five solves; the range is the spread across those five.
Best of five solves; the range is the spread across those five.
.PP
Read the per-iteration columns.
Below mesh twenty-eight the GPU's cost is flat whatever the problem size
\(em 176 to 200 microseconds across a sevenfold range \(em which is what
the per-call cost of crossing this interface looks like, while the CPU's
grows with the problem, which is what arithmetic looks like.
They cross per matvec at 28\(mu28\(mu28, 250 against 222, and per solve
at 32\(mu32\(mu32, 17ms against 15ms.
.PP
Two cautions on that per-solve figure.
It is f32 against f64 and takes 52 iterations against 45, so the two
columns are not the same solve; and the GPU's spread is far wider
(15\(en28ms against 17\(en18ms), so the win is on the best of five and
not on the worst.
.PP
This table said for some time that there was no break-even at any size
that would fit.
That was not a fact about the machine: pool sizes were parsed into a C
.BR int ,
so
.B -pheap=4g
arrived as zero and anything from 2GB up was refused as "not enough
memory".
A mesh of twenty-eight cubed needs a few megabytes.
.PP
Two things are needed to get figures worth reading, and
.IR gpubench (1)
now does both.
A GPU solve has to be run and discarded first, or the one-time cost of
setting up Metal lands on whichever solve runs first and is divided by
that mesh's iteration count \(em which made mesh twelve report 74ms
instead of 4ms and look more expensive than mesh twenty-four.
And the solve has to be repeated, because it takes single-figure
milliseconds on a millisecond clock and the GPU figure varies by a third
between runs where the CPU figure does not.
An earlier version of this table was one sample per size and its
per-iteration numbers were noise.
Note that f32 also costs iterations \(em 39 against 34 at
24\(mu24\(mu24 \(em so the two columns are not the same solve, and any
comparison drawn from them should carry both counts.
.PP
This table used to show the GPU winning above about nine thousand nodes.
Nothing about this device changed; the baseline did.
.IR sparse (2)
now computes its matvec through
.IR math (2)'s
.B spmv
builtin instead of a loop in Limbo, which is nine to ten times faster,
and an offload measured against an interpreted loop was measuring the
interpreter.
Reducing the per-call cost is what would make this device pay, and it
has not been attempted.
.SS "Where the time goes"
Setting
.B INFERNO_METAL_STATS
makes each matvec's cost be reported every two hundred calls, split into
converting the vector in, encoding the command buffer, committing and
waiting for it, and converting the result out.
.PP
The answer is not what one would guess and it decides whether using this
device is worth doing:
.IP
.EX
mesh      convert in   encode   commit+wait   convert out
24x24x24     2.6us      4.2us      194.2us       2.0us
32x32x32     4.5us      4.3us      211.3us       4.4us
.EE
.PP
The commit and wait is 94% of the call and barely moves while the problem
grows nearly threefold.
The cost of this interface is a command-buffer round trip, not data
movement \(em so making the transfers cheaper, or keeping vectors on the
device, addresses the ten microseconds and not the two hundred.
.PP
It also says where the break-even comes from: the GPU wins exactly when a
matvec on the CPU costs more than about 200 microseconds, which is mesh
twenty-eight.
Making this device pay more generally means issuing fewer command
buffers, and for a Krylov solve that means running the whole iteration on
the GPU, since each iteration depends on the one before and they cannot
be batched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B INFERNO_NOGPUHW
Leaves the platform's hardware backend unregistered, so every matvec uses
this device's own portable CPU loop.
The point is comparison: it makes the two paths differ by nothing but the
backend, inside one binary and one environment, which is otherwise impossible
\(em
.B emu-g
has no Metal at all, so it differs in far more than the backend.
That is how the corruption described in
.B /doc/hpc-plan.md
was localised.
